WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.905
A shopper presses Place Order and expects one purchase confirmation. The client

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:11.810
should create one stable checkout identity before sending the request.

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:18.180
Kubernetes starts replacing checkout pod A. Readiness updates and connection draining need

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:24.110
time to move new traffic away while existing requests finish.

00:00:24.542 --> 00:00:30.987
A stale gateway endpoint or pooled connection still reaches pod A. The

00:00:30.987 --> 00:00:37.432
shopper sees an error. Readiness-aware routing and draining protect this path.

00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:41.610
A later retry reaches pod B and saves the order, but its

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.387
reply is lost. A timeout does not prove that the database rolled

00:00:45.387 --> 00:00:49.163
back.

00:00:49.583 --> 00:00:56.508
The shopper retries the same purchase. Reuse the original customer-scoped idempotency key

00:00:56.508 --> 00:01:03.433
and compare the payload. A fresh identity can create a duplicate order.

00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:08.730
Route new traffic to ready pod B, and return the original saved

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:13.627
result for the same request. Draining protects routing. Durable idempotency protects one

00:01:13.627 --> 00:01:18.523
logical purchase.
